# UX-SPEC.md §3.6 (tab strip, `+` menu, tab menu, close ✕) and §5.1 rule 6 — every tab verb has a
# pointer twin of its `^s` key: the `+` opens a menu whose *Terminal* row is `^s c`, the ✕ is
# `^s x`, and a right-click selects the tab before its menu opens. §3.1 for the breadcrumb.
#
# `one-repo` opens on the three default windows: two PTYs, then Fleet's own git pane.
fixture: one-repo
await idle
key o
await screen == Workspace 20000
await lists.tabs.rows[2] exists 20000
await lists.tabs.selected.id == 1 20000
assert targets["workspace.back"] exists && targets["workspace.switcher"] exists
assert targets["tabs.new"] exists && targets["tabs.zoom"] exists
shot workspace-strip

# The worktree switcher lists the sessions as `^s W` does, this one checked.
click workspace.switcher
await targets["menu.item[0]"] exists
shot worktree-switcher
key escape
await targets["menu.item[0]"] absent

# `+` → Terminal is `^s c`: a fourth tab, selected, like the key.
click tabs.new
await targets["menu.item[0]"] exists
shot new-tab-menu
click menu.item[0]
await lists.tabs.rows[3] exists 20000
await lists.tabs.selected.id == 4 20000
assert focused == tabs.tab[3]

# A right-click selects the tab under it and opens its menu: Rename, Close, Close others.
click right tabs.tab[1]
await lists.tabs.selected.id == 2 10000
await targets["menu.item[2]"] exists
shot tab-menu
key escape
await targets["menu.item[0]"] absent

# The ✕ on the active tab is `^s x`: the plain shell has no keep-alive process, so it closes
# at once, with no confirm.
click tabs.tab[3]
await lists.tabs.selected.id == 4 10000
click tabs.tab[3].close
await lists.tabs.rows[3] absent 10000
assert screen == Workspace && overlay absent
await idle
dump tabs-by-pointer
quit
